# Untitled

## Applications of pillarenes, an emerging class of synthetic macrocycles
SONG Nan & YANG Ying-Wei
State Key Laboratory of Supramolecular Structure and Materials, College of Chemistry, Jilin University, Changchun 130012, China
Synthetic macrocycles, a typical type of building block for molecular recognition and self-assembly, are crucial to supramolecular chemistry and materials science. Since 2008, a new generation of synthetic macrocyclic hosts, pillarenes and their abundant derivatives, which consist of hydroquinone units linked by methylene bridges at 2,5-positions, have been the focus of much research. Numerous studies on their host-guest properties and the fabrication of supramolecular assemblies have demonstrated that pillarenes and their derivatives possess many advantages that facilitate their applications in many research fields. Herein we summarize and classify the applications of pillarenes in terms of artificial transmembrane channels, controlled delivery systems, dispersion of carbon hybrid materials, extraction and absorption, liquid crystals, metal-organic frameworks, sensing and detection, stabilization of nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Au/Ag/CdTe})$ , and other typical biological applications. We also provide an overview of future developments in pillarene chemistry.
drug delivery, host-guest interactions, macrocycles, pillarenes, supramolecular materials
## 1 Introduction
Nature possesses a fascinating ability to construct sophisticated and precise biological systems with multiple functions. Supramolecular interactions are significant in biosystems [1]. Non-covalent interactions, which are at the core of supramolecular chemistry, have uniquely reversible characteristics compared with covalent interactions; they also pave the way for the fabrication of functional supramolecular materials that are reversibly responsive to environmental stimulation [2]. Synthetic functional macrocycles, as the foundational building blocks of supramolecular chemistry, have attracted considerable attention due to their intriguing features [3]. They can capture guest molecules into their vacant cavities through various non-covalent interactions that also construct special supramolecular architectures, including rotaxanes, catenanes and topological gels [4].
A new generation of synthetic supramolecular macrocycles, known as pillarenes [5], first appeared in the literature in 2008 [6]. These new macrocycles have been widely studied and rapidly developed because they possess simple symmetric rigid structures and are facile to be functionalized with multifarious substituents. The basic research into pillarenes has centered on the pursuit of high-yield synthetic routes [7, 8], host-guest properties with different guest molecules [9–11], and supramolecular assembly [12, 13]. The morphologies of pillarene-based assemblies, which varies from zero dimensional to three dimensional, includes vesicles [14], micelles [15], linear polymers [16], fibers [17], nanotubes [18], micro-rings [19] and multiple layers [20] (Figure 1). Various stimulations for driving the complexation and decomplexation between pillarenes and their corresponding guests [21] have also been widely developed; these include $\mathrm{pH}$ , thermal/solvent response, UV-Vis irradiation stimulation, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -responsive systems [22]. Supramolecular assemblies and disassemblies can be achieved with these external stimuli. Furthermore, based on the research about self-assembled architectures and typical reversible stimulations, extensive applications have been developed since 2012 in many areas based on pillarene derivatives [23]. Pillarene-based supramolecular materials have been utilized as artificial transmembrane channels and controlled delivery systems, and for the dispersion of carbon hybrid materials as well as extraction and absorption, liquid crystals, metal-organic frameworks, sensing and detection, stabilization of nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Au/Ag/CdTe})$ , and other typical biological applications. In this review, representative applications of pillarenes from the above aspects will be discussed.
## 2  Applications of pillarenes
## 2.1 Artificial transmembrane channels
Four types of artificial transmembrane channels designed from typical functionalized pillarenes have been reported by Hou et al. They can efficiently mediate the transport of protons, water, amino acids, and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions. The first pillarenebased artificial transmembrane channels, which were reported in 2011 [24], were the first examples of water-wirebased proton channels built from synthetic systems.
The second example involves single-molecule artificial transmembrane water channels [25]. Synthesized hydrazideappended pillar[5]arene (P5) derivatives were endowed with a unique tubular conformation. Dynamic light scattering (DLS) and cryogenic scanning electron microscopy (cryo-SEM) were used to investigate the water transport properties. An obvious decrease in light scattering was attributed to the water transport from outside to inside the vesicles. The channel-open probability of the P5 dimer was $64\%$ , more than three times higher than for monomeric P5.
The third instance of the artificial channel (Figure 2(a)) is constructed from peptide-appended pillar[n]arene ${\it\Delta}n=5$ , 6;
i.e., P5 and P6) derivatives, which can be used for the chiral selective transmembrane transport of amino acids [26]. Experimental results indicated tubular conformation in solution and lipid bilayer membranes. The property of the tubular molecules for Gly transport was explored via the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR experiments. The appearance of a small peak at 171.7 ppm corresponding to the $\bar{\mathrm{O}}{=}^{13}\mathrm{C}$ signal of Gly loaded in the vesicles demonstrated the transmembrane transport of Gly over the course of $80~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 2(b)). Thus, the efficient transport of amino acids across lipid membranes at a very low channel-to-lipid ratio $(\mathrm{EC}50~=~0.002~\mathrm{~mol}\%)$ was achieved. Furthermore, chiral selectivity was also realized, which mediates the new way of mimicking the key functions of natural amino acid channels.
A new type of artificial transmembrane channel was synthesized by attaching positively charged Arg-incorporated tripeptide chains to P5 [27]. Significantly, the authors have introduced voltage as a driving force to impel the artificial channel molecules entering and leaving a lipid bilayer and thereby achieved $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ion transportation through the bilayer (Figure 3). Polarized large unilamellar vesicles (LUVs) made from egg yolk L-a-phosphatidylcholine (EYPC) were used to investigate the depolarized ability of 4a–d and thus the possibility of crossing the bilayer. The EYPC-LUVs possess KCl inside, NaCl outside, and Safranin O as a fluorescence probe to detect the membrane potential. As shown in Figure 3, upon the addition of $\mathbf{4a-d}$ , the polarized bilayer was depolarized due to the inserted molecules, which allowed the external $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ or $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions to flux inside the vesicle. Such results were demonstrated by the significant fluorescence intensity decrease of Safranin O (by $28\%$ , $63\%$ , $51\%$ , and $50\%$ ). Patch-clamp experiments at different alternate pulses indicated recyclable entry and egress of the channel molecules. When the voltage was increased from 0 to $-140\mathrm{mV}$ , pillarene-based channel molecules have shown different voltage-responsive behaviors; the higher gating charges $(z_{\mathrm{g}})$ of 4c indicate that it is more voltage-sensitive than $\mathbf{4b}$ and 4d. Furthermore, 4c exhibited antimicrobial activity toward Bacillus subtilis whereas 4b and 4d did not.
## 2.2 Controlled-delivery systems
Research about pillarenes has rapidly moved from synthesis to applications due to the diverse structures and typical cavity sizes of these materials. Because of their desirable host-guest properties, pillarenes are regarded as attractive candidates not only for the design and synthesis of nanovalve systems but also for the fabrication of special cargorelease systems such as vesicles (Figures 4 and 5).
In 2013 we reported a water-soluble carboxylatopillar[5] arene (WP5)-based (Figure 4) on-command cargo-release system (Figure 5(a)) [28]. Due to their high surface areas, low cytotoxicity, appropriate sizes, and good biocompatibility, mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSNs) were designed as the vehicles to load cargos. Considering the electron-rich cavity of WP5, positively charged pyridinium moieties showing efficient binding affinity toward WP5 $\prime K_{\mathrm{a}}=(2.5\pm$ $0.7)\times10^{3}~\mathrm{L/mol})$ were grafted onto the surfaces of MSNs, followed by capping with WP5 molecules. In this way, a new cargo release system was constructed in which the controlled release of cargos could be achieved by not only changing the environmental $\mathrm{pH}$ but also adding competitive binding agents. Experimental results demonstrated that the cargo molecules (i.e., RhB) could be trapped and stored in the pores of MSNPs under neutral and basic conditions. However, when the $\mathrm{\pH}$ is lowered, the cargo RhB molecules will be released due to the uncapping of the pores (Figure 5(b)). The lower the $\mathrm{\pH}$ , the faster the release. We also investigated the release of an anticancer drug, doxorubicin (DOX) hydrochloride as well as another method to achieve the cargo release (i.e., competitive binding activation). With the inclusion of methyl viologen salts, which have a higher binding affinity $(K_{\mathrm{a}}=8\times10^{-4}~\mathrm{L/mol})$ toward WP5, the release of RhB or calcein molecules was observed.
Vesicles can be constructed from amphiphilic molecules. The hollow center of the vesicle makes it a potential container for small molecules. Huang et al. [14] reported the complexation between water-soluble carboxylatopillar[6] arene salts (WP6) and an amphiphilic paraquat derivative (Figure 5(c)). First, the association constant of WP6 and paraquat (G1, Figure 4) was measured by fluorescence titration experiments. The $K_{\mathrm{a}}$ value was $(1.02\pm0.10)\times10^{8}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mol}$ , which indicated a very stable host-guest binding motif of $_{\mathrm{G1}\subset\mathrm{WP6}}$ . The amphiphilic paraquat derivative itself (G2, Figure 4) can assemble into micelles in water. However, the micelles change to vesicles upon addition of WP6 due to the host-guest inclusion complexation between WP6 and paraquat. Due to the reversible pH-responsiveness of WP6, the transition of micelles and vesicles can be achieved by adjusting the pH. The calcein molecules can be loaded into the hollow cavities of vesicles, after which the controlled release is finally realized via the pH-responsive vesicles-micelles transformation.
Another drug delivery system (Figure 5(f)) based on supramolecular vesicles was reported by Wang et al. [29]. The vesicles were developed through the assembly of the same water-soluble pillarene (i.e., WP6) and the hydrophobic ferrocene derivative (G3, Figure 4) in water. As is customary, the binding affinity between WP6 and ferrocene derivative in water was determined by UV-Vis spectroscopy to be $(1.27\pm0.42)\times10^{5}\mathrm{L/mol}$ with 1:1 binding stoichiometry. The encapsulation and controlled release of the anticancer drug mitoxantrone (MTZ) was investigated based on the supramolecular vesicles. The MTZ-loaded supramolecular vesicles can rapidly release MTZ in low-pH environment due to the disassembly of the vesicles. The high efficiency of this MTZ-loaded release system suggests its potential in the applications of intracellular drug delivery and cancer treatment.
## 2.3  Dispersion of carbon hybrid materials
In recent years, many active investigations have focused on carbon nanotechnologies. In fact, only carbon as a single element can form so many types of materials with completely different structures and properties, such as 3D diamond crystal, 2D graphene sheets, 1D carbon nanotubes, and zero-dimensional fullerene molecules. Within the variety of carbon nanomaterials that have emerged, nanotubes have shown particular utility as well as versatility. Among the advantages of carbon nanotubes (CNTs) are high elasticity, low density, good insulation, high strength, infrared absorption, and invisibility under infrared and electromagnetic waves. For these reasons, they have great potential in military and astronautic equipment.
However, the lack of solubility of CNTs in solvents challenges their applications. The Huang group [30] developed a method based on water-soluble P6 to disperse multiwalled CNTs (MWNTs) in water (Figure 6) through non-covalent interactions. Because functionalization on the sidewalls of CNTs through supramolecular approaches cannot influence their unique properties, pyrene derivatives have been designed to disperse CNTs in solvents. Because the solubility remains difficult to control, a pyrene derivative has been synthesized that is functionalized with a neutral guest group; it shows an efficient binding affinity $\mathit{\Omega}(8.04\pm0.68)\times10^{4}$ $\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mol})$ to the hydrophobic cavity of WP6. The $\pi$ -rich pyrene groups can attach on the sidewall surfaces of MWNTs through $\pi-\pi$ interactions and, at the same time, the neutral groups on the opposite end can complex with WP6 dispersed in water. As expected, the MWNTs were well dispersed in water in an aqueous solution of $_{\mathrm{G4}\mathrm{{cWP6}}}$ , instead of in G4 alone (Figure 7). Because the host-guest complexation and water solubility of WP6 can be controlled by adjusting the solution $\mathrm{\pH}$ , the dispersion of MWNTs is also easily controllable and reversible. As shown in Figure 6, when the solution is acidic $\mathrm{(pH}<7\AA)$ , water-soluble $-\mathrm{COO}^{-}$ changes into insoluble –COOH; the host-guest interaction between WP6 and G4 is destroyed; and MWNTs are precipitated. When the solution is basic $\mathrm{(pH>7)}$ ), MWNTs can again be well dispersed in water and can stand for $>1$ month with negligible changes. The pH-controlled dispersion of MWNTs can be observed from TEM images as well as photographs.
Based on the above study, Huang et al. [31] investigated another dispersion system of MWNTs. The pyrenes attach to the surface of MWNTs through $\pi\cdot\pi$ interactions, whereas the MWNTs are soluble in water in consequence of the hydrophilic WP6. Unlike the previous work, the guest pyrene derivative (G5, Figure 7) possesses photocleavage property and the destruction of MWNTs dispersion in water is achieved by UV irradiation due to the photocleavage between hydrophilic segment and carbon hybrids. Thus, UV-responsive dispersion of MWNTs has been arranged.
Reduced-graphene oxide (RGO) is another type of carbon material with large planar structure and remarkable electronic properties, but slight solubility. By decorating an amphiphilic P5 (AP5) onto the surface of RGO, Diao et al. [32] designed and synthesized the pillarene-based waterdispersive RGO nanocomposite (RGO-AP5) (Figure 8). The mechanism of assembly between AP5 and RGO can be contributed to the covalent bond forces of amido bonds, reacted from amino and carboxyl groups, as well as the $\pi\cdot\pi$ noncovalent force between the benzene rings and surfaces of RGO. In addition, Au nanoparticles have been assembled onto the surface of RGO-AP5 through amido groups to construct ternary nanocomposites (RGO-AP5-AuNPs). RGOAP5-AuNPs include all the advantages of their three components: excellent conductivity and large surface area of RGO, selective recognition to typical molecules of AP5, and catalytic property of AuNPs, all of which enhance the electrochemical response for guest materials. As a result, RGO-AP5-AuNPs exhibits an excellent electrochemical analyzing performance for dopamine (DA) with broad linear range $\cdot1.5\times10^{-8}$ to $1.9\times10^{-5}$ mol/L) and very low detection concentration $(1.2\times10^{-8}\mathrm{mol/L})$ ) at a signal-to-noise ratio of 3.
In short, the dispersion of carbon materials based on P5 derivatives extends their technological applications in many areas.
## 2.4  Extraction and adsorption
In 2013, in collaboration with the Jia group, we [33] reported $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles functionalized with neutral carboxylatopillar[5]arene (CP[5]A) through covalent bonds, which can be used as magnetic solid-phase extraction absorbents for pesticides from beverages, in combination with high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, Figure 9(a)). The $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C P[5]A}$ composite material has been fully characterized by sufficient methods including SEM, TEM, etc. Its magnetic properties have been particularly investigated by a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM, Figure 9(b)). The $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C P[5]A}$ nanoparticles (Figures 9(c, d)) have been observed to be strongly superparamagnetic in the absence of hysteresis and in the presence of negligible remanence and coercivity. The rapid isolation of nanoparticles from turbid dispersion was also seen after the nanoparticles were treated with an external NdFeB magnetic field (Figure 9(b)). After optimization of a magnetic solid-phase extraction (MSPE) procedure, an efficient and convenient method for the detection of seven pesticides-metalaxyl, dimethomorph, kresoxim-methyl, flusilazole, cyprodinil, pyrimethanil, and triflumizole, in beverage samples, was successfully developed, accompanied by HPLC-UV. This method, which combined the magnetic property of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with rapid analysis of HPLC, as well as the pillar shape and rigid macrocyclic structures of pillarenes, has shown higher efficiency of absorbence and extraction of pesticides than traditional MSPE.
The separation of actinide and trivalent lanthanide elements is a crucial and urgent problem in nuclear spent-fuel reprocessing. Because the coordination numbers of lanthanides and actinides are very high, selective separation can be realized by introducing multiple dentate on specific molecular skeletons. Yuan et al. [34] reported the first example of an extraction method based on pillarenes functionalized with diglycolamides (DGAs, Figure 10), a wellknown multidentate extractant of actinides from acidicwaste solutions. DGAs-substituted P5s as extractants have exhibited greatly efficient extraction and separation toward the partitioning of $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ and $\mathbf{Am}(\mathrm{III})$ at feed acidities. The practical importance lies in the extraction abilities in highly acidic solution and the multiple extraction of $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ and $\mathbf{Am}(\mathrm{III})$ . The rigid and symmetric structures of pillarenes make them very different from calixarene extractants in ways that may realize potential extraction of $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ and $\mathbf{Am}(\mathrm{III})$ from radioactive nuclear waste remediation. Continuing the above work, Yuan’s group [35] designed a P5 derivative with ten phosphine oxides as chelating groups that is capable of selectively extracting thorium(IV) and uranyl(VI).
The yellow explosive 2,4,6-trinitrotoluene (TNT) is significantly harmful to the environment and human health. Huang et al. [36] reported an amphiphilic P5 derivative from a pre-synthesized amphiphilic monomer M1 (Figure 11). These amphiphilic pillarenes can assemble into vesicles and subsequently fabricate to multiwalled macrotubes with the ability to absorb TNT. The absorption is driven by donor-acceptor interactions (i.e., the multiwalled macrotubes with electron-rich surfaces can absorb electron-poor TNT). The monomer was not able to assemble into macrotubes or absorb TNT, however, which indicates the significant effect of rigid pillarene frames.
Huang et al. [37] also prepared macroporous/mesoporous polymeric materials that were successfully applied to the selective absorption of $n$ -alklyene diols, carboxylic acidsubstituted P5s, and poly(ionic liquid) fabricated to the polymetric material by in situ ionic complexation in organic solvents. The solution-state host-guest property of P5 provides the material with affinities to designated molecules, while the spongelike superstructure with interstitial secondary pores improves liquid transport. The porous hybrid material can absorb a series of aliphatic diols from $\mathrm{CDCl}_{3}$ ; of these, it has shown the highest affinity to 1,4-butanediol (Figure 12).
Recently, we also packed ten carboxyl-substituted watersoluble P5 derivatives (CP[5]A) in a glass microcolumn [38], which acted as absorbent for separation and preconcentration of trace-level noble metals such as gold (Au) and palladium (Pd), combined with flow injection-flame atomic absorption spectrometry (FAAS).
## 2.5 Liquid crystals
The first liquid-crystalline P5 derivative (LP5) was synthesized by decorating mesomophic subunits (i.e, mesogetic cyanobiphenyl units) on the whole rims of the macrocyclic core [39] (Figure 13). Compared with its corresponding model compound that bears only two mesomorphic groups (M1) without the rigid cavity, the P5-based liquid crystalline displays excellent mesomorphic properties due to its unique pentagonal rigid structure. Polarized optical microscopy (POM) has indicated typical liquid-crystalline morphologies (Figure 14(c)); its thermal properties were investigated by differential scanning calorimetry (DSC). It is obvious that the liquid crystals with rigid pentagonal cores result in an enantiotropic smectic A phase over a broad temperature range (Tg: $32^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; $\mathrm{SmA}\rightarrow\mathrm{I};201^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\Delta H=53$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ ; Figure 14(b)), whereas the model compound reveals only a monotropic smectic A phase $\mathrm{(I\to\SmA}$ : $149~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\Delta H=5.8~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ ; $\mathrm{SmA}\ \to\ \mathrm{Cr}$ : $125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\Delta H=119~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ ;
Figure 14(a)). The macrocyclic core provides the orientational and positional disorder within the smectic layer and simultaneously prevents crystal formation through $\pi-\pi$ interactions between neighboring cores. The influence of the macrocyclic core can be summarized as follows: prevention of crystallization and stabilizing of the smectic A phase through $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between neighboring pillarene cores, which can be observed by the broad enantiotropic mesophase.
Following the previous literature, in order to exploit the effects of macrocyclic cores on mesomorphic properties, liquid-crystalline P5 and P6 bearing ten or twelve mesomorphic cyanobiphenyl moieties respectively have been synthesized [40]. All of the results have shown that mesophase of both P5 and P6 is stable over a greatly broader temperature range than for model compounds, a stability that results from the rigid macrocyclic cores. There is also no difference in the supramolecular arrangement between liquid crystalline P5 and P6, in which each smetic layer is interdigitated into two neighboring layers. In their smectic A phase, glide can occur between adjacent layers that are rigid in a vertical direction. The high degree of interpenetration of layer-by-layer structure produces good filling in space and an efficient lateral arrangement of the mesogenic units. The chiral property of pillarene derivatives was also observed.
## 2.6  Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)
Stoddart and coworkers reported the first example of MOFs consisting of rigid pillarene-based ligands [41]. The MOFs had an active domain that expressed good and selective recognition of neutral electron-poor aromatic guests such as 1,4-dinitrobenzene, as well as positively charged pyridinium and viologen guests. The uptake of guests induced color changes in the cubic crystals from faint yellow to deep orange, which indicated charge transfer between the guests and the active domains of the pillarene moieties in the MOFs.
Homochiral MOFs (Figure 15) containing enantiopure P5-active domains have been constructed by Stoddart et al. [42]. These homochiral MOFs were prepared by linking enantiopure struts containing P5s that incorporated planar chirality together with $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ clusters. Intriguingly, racemic pillarene derivatives were also resolved in the preparation of homochiral MOFs. These homochiral active domains are potentially useful as solid supports in chiral chromatography.
## 2.7  Sensing and detection
Fluorescence as the output signal is a quick and convenient method for the detection of typical molecules. The first fluorescence pillarene-based sensor to alkanediamines, constructed from a pyrene-substituted P5 derivative, was reported by Stoddart et al. [43] in 2011. Subsequently, Wang et al. [44] reported polypseudorotaxanes that could serve as efficient anion-responsive fluorescence sensors. As shown in Figure 16, these researchers first prepared a P5 dimer (DP5) and then investigated the complexation property between DP5 and the guest molecule, $n$ -octylpyrazinium hexafluorophosphate (G6). The average association constant $\left(K_{\mathrm{av}}\right)$ was estimated to be $(3.5\pm0.{\overset{\cdot}{2}})\times10^{2}\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mol}$ . The polymer P1, that was synthesized from pillarene derivative M1, can form novel side-chain polypseudorotaxanes through the strong host-guest affinities upon addition of G6. Intriguingly, bare P1 shows strong emission at $445~\mathrm{nm}$ in chloroform whereas the fluorescence emission intensity decreases gradually upon addition of G6. This phenomenon can be ascribed to the efficient electron transfer from the conjugated backbone of P1 side-chain to the cation moiety of G6, which leads to the fluorescence quenching. The chloride anion effects have also been investigated because of the tight ion pair formation between chloride anion and $n$ -octylpyrazinium cation that switches off the complexation of P1 and G6. As expected, the fluorescence emission intensity of the polypseudorotaxanes of P1 and G6 enhanced upon addition of TBACl. The fluorescence enhancement was also easily distinguished by the naked eye under
UV-light irradiation. All of these results demonstrated that synthesized side-chain polypseudorotaxanes could be regarded as novel anion-responsive fluorescence sensors.
Further exploitation of these novel pillarene-based polypseudorotaxanes as the fluorescence sensors has been reported by the same group [45]. The halogen ions can be selectively detected via different fluorescence emission intensities. Huang et al. [46] reported two kinds of fluorescence-based sensors. The first one was a host-guest complexation-induced emission system based on water-soluble P6 and a tetraphenylethene (TPE)-derivative guest (Figure 17). The tetrameric complexation of WP6 and TPE ammonium salt derivative results in strong emission enhancement in dilute solution because the bulky rigid P6 of pseudorotaxane restricts the intermolecular rotation efficiently and facilitates the radiative decay. Furthermore, the tetrameric host-guest system can serve as a probe to detect paraquat accompanied by fluorescence switching off, due to the competitive binding between WP6 and paraquat.
The second fluorescence sensor reported by the Huang group [47] is a bola-type supra-amphiphile constructed from a water-soluble P5 and a rod-coil molecule. Addition of paraquat or decrease of $\mathsf{p H}$ will weaken the fluorescence emission intensity of this bola-type supra-amphiphile, which indicates its potential to act as a dual fluorescence sensor that is responsive to both paraquat and pH.
Many other types of sensors that are responsive to other stimulus have also been exploited. For example, $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR-based biosensors were constructed by favoring the solubility of xenon in water through the complexation between a water-soluble P5 and the xenon atom, as reported by Cohen et al [48]. A low $\mathrm{pH}$ sensor at the range of $\mathrm{pH}1$ to 4 formed by an esterified P5 has been reported by Cragg et al. [49]. Recently, a novel, reversible, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -responsive P5 was successfully prepared by Huang et al. [19]. The turbid solution after bubbling with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be distinguished with the naked eye from clarified solution after treatment with ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
## 2.8 Stabilization of nanoparticles (Au/Ag/CdTe)
Water-soluble P5s containing ten imidazolium entities were first used to stabilize Au nanoparticles by Huang et al. [50]. The imidazolium groups can bind to the gold surface through weakly coordinating cations and anions. Thus the plasmon resonance of the stabilized gold nanoparticles exhibits greater sensitivity than nanoparticles stabilized by the commonly employed capping or protective ligands; this difference indicates the superior activity as gold nanoparticle catalysts.
Our group [51] reported the synthesis of WP5-modified gold nanoparticles (Figure 18). WP5 acted as a stabilizer for in situ preparation of gold nanoparticles with good dispersion and narrow size distributions $(3.1\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) in aqueous solutions. Supramolecular self-assembly of WP5-modified gold nanoparticles was observed and mediated by introducing suitable guest molecules. Significantly, the new hybrid materials can serve as a useful system for sensing and detecting of herbicide paraquat.
WP5-stabilized silver nanoparticles were synthesized by Huang and coworkers [52, 53], via reducing ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ (10 mmol/L) with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ $(50\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ in the presence of a series of selected concentrations of WP5. The diameters of WP5-stabilized silver nanoparticles were influenced by the concentration of WP5, which could be observed by the surface plasmon resonance (SPR) of silver nanoparticles. Due to the increasing concentration of WP5, the SPR peak gradually underwent a blue shift from 430 to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ , indicating smaller average silver nanoparticle diameters.
WP5s were modified on the surface of colloidal semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) via a ligand-exchange method by us [54]. QDs have been successfully used in biological sensing, immunoassays, and biomacromolecular fluorescence labeling and biological imaging in the past decades. The successful modification indicates the ability of WP5s to stabilize QDs. Combing the properties of QDs and pillarenes, the newly prepared organic-inorganic nanocomposite showed great potential as a candidate for the detection of herbicide, i.e., paraquat through photophisical property changes. Meanwhile, the nanocomposite can also form an aggregated network through supramolecular interactions between WP5s on the surface and the bridged guests.
## 2.9 Other typical biological applications
Pillarenes also have exhibited typical biological applications other than drug delivery for disease treatments [55, 56]. In 2013, Nierengarten and coworkers [57] designed and synthesized a mannosylated P5 derivative, a P5 glycocluster that can serve as inhibitor against uropathogenic bacteria. They also showed that polycationic P5 derivatives could interact with DNA for biological applications [58].
In collaboration with the Wu group, we [59] reported that WP5 could work as an efficient inhibitor for the formation of the human papillomavirus 16 (HPV 16) L1 pentamer, because WP5 can bind to L-arginine (Arg) and L-lysine (Lys) at the monomer interface. The major capsid protein of HPV, L1, is rich with Arg and Lys residues. In addition, the capsid proteins can self-assemble into pentamers and other, viruslike particles (VLPs). VLPs are formed via the interactions between helices 2 and 4 of L1, where they linked to each other via the monomer interface of L1. Thus, creating inhibitors toward L1 pentamer formation and preventing further virus formation is a particularly efficient antiviral method. After gradual treatment of glutathione-S-transferase (GST) fusion L1 (i.e., GST-L1) with WP5, the rate of pentamer formation were efficiently inhibited. Nevertheless, when WP5 was removed by dialysis, the cleaved L1 could form L1 pentamer and further VLPs. Similarly inhibatory behavior can be observed by sulfonatocalix[4]arene (SC4A), which suggests that WP5 has better efficiency in inhibiting L1 pentamer formation. This work obviously demonstrated the potential of pillarene derivatives in prophylactics and therapies for HPV infections.
Zhao et al. [60] reported four types of amphiphilic P5 derivatives for self-assembling to biocompatible vesicles (Figure 19) that can deliver dyes into cells and achieve dual bioimaging in vitro. Vesicles assembled from different types of amphiphilic pillarenes exhibit very different behaviors for dye delivery. All types of assemblies can deliver hydrophilic RhB, but only tadpole-like amphiphilic pillarene assembly can deliver fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) well enough for bioimaging. After complexation with electron-accepter guest G, pillarenes 1, 3, and 4 can further assemble into vesicles for bioimaging. Tadpole-like pillarene 1 has been shown to exhibit best the performance in dye delivery and bioimaging.
The Zhao group [61] provided us with prepared P5- modified graphene oxides (GO). This kind of hybrid material can perform as an excellent candidate for bioimaging that consists of Raman and fluorescence imaging in vitro. We also reported a new type of carboxylatopillarene- modified RGO with high water dispersibility, exhibiting a good fluorescent dye sensing ability [62].
## 3  Conclusions and outlook
This review summarized the advanced applications of pillarenes in recent years. Much foundational research has been done on pillarenes, including efficient synthetic methods, host-guest properties, stimuli-responsive properties, and supramolecular self-assemblies. The tremendous significance of pillarenes is exhibited via their particular performances, which are reflected in their applications in artificial transmembrane channels, controlled delivery systems, dispersion of carbon hybrid materials, extraction and absorption, liquid crystals, metal-organic frameworks, sensing and detection, stabilization of nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Au/Ag/CdTe})$ , and other typical biological applications. Pillarenes, as the newest generation of macrocycles, are still facing challenges as well as opportunities for further investigation, because of their unique architectures and diverse functionalizations. With the further development of science and technologies, their applications in numerous other fields will continue to become evident [63–65]. First, facile synthetic methods of diverse pillarene derivatives will be designed to provide basic ingredients for various utilization demands. Second, considering that pillarene research is still in its infancy because of cavity-size limitations, strategies to improve the yields of P6s, P7s, and other pillarenes with larger cavity sizes must be discovered so as to develop pillarenes’ host-guest properties and further supramolecular assemblies. Third, much more attention should be focused on stimuli responsiveness and smart supramolecular materials in terms of controllable self-assemblies. Fourth, high-order pillarenes with special functions, such as trimers and tetramers, should be prepared for fabrication of sophisticated structures. Finally, expansion of pillarene applications in biosystems is also required. Many pillarene derivatives cannot be dissolved in water, which restricts their applications in biology. Therefore, preparing special structure of pillarenes with great biocompatibility has a bright future in biosensors and disease therapies. Further investigation of pillarenes and derivatives will usher supramolecular chemistry into a new era. It is expected that the applications and potentials of this new type of macrocycle will provide new understandings of supramolecular chemistry.
